---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Quiz from '../../components/Quiz.astro';

const questions = [
  {
    question: "What is the first question this pill says to ask before designing a streaming system?",
    options: [
      "Which cloud provider offers the cheapest storage",
      "Does this pipeline need aggregates over time, like moving averages or windowed counts",
      "How many engineers are available to build it",
      "Which programming language the team prefers"
    ],
    correct: 1,
    explanation: "The first fork in the road is whether the use case needs state at all. Everything else in the checklist only matters if the answer is yes."
  },
  {
    question: "If the answer to question 1 is 'no', what does this pill recommend?",
    options: [
      "Build a full Flink cluster regardless, to future-proof the system",
      "A simple stateless setup like Lambda or a scheduled script is genuinely fine",
      "Always default to Kafka Streams",
      "Redesign the use case until it needs state"
    ],
    correct: 1,
    explanation: "If there is no need for memory across events, reaching for a stateful streaming engine would be unnecessary complexity. A stateless setup is the right tool there."
  },
  {
    question: "What does question 2 (tolerance for late data) decide?",
    options: [
      "Whether to use tumbling or sliding windows only",
      "Whether a simple drop-and-log policy is enough, or the more expensive reopen-and-correct approach is required",
      "How many partitions the topic should have",
      "Whether the pipeline needs a schema registry"
    ],
    correct: 1,
    explanation: "The business's tolerance for occasional undercounts is what decides between a cheap drop policy and a more expensive update-and-correct policy."
  },
  {
    question: "Who should answer question 2, according to this pill?",
    options: [
      "The business, since it is a decision about acceptable risk, not an engineering preference",
      "Whoever is on call that week",
      "The cloud provider's default settings",
      "The original author of the source system"
    ],
    correct: 0,
    explanation: "This is a risk and cost decision, not a technical one, so it belongs with whoever owns the business impact of the metric being wrong."
  },
  {
    question: "What does question 3 (clock reliability) ask you to check before trusting event-time windows?",
    options: [
      "Whether the topic has enough partitions",
      "The gap between when the server received an event and when the device says it sent it, to calibrate for drift",
      "Whether the consumer group has committed its offsets",
      "Whether the database supports foreign keys"
    ],
    correct: 1,
    explanation: "Device clocks are not always trustworthy, so measuring the offset between server receive time and device send time lets you calibrate event time before relying on it."
  },
  {
    question: "According to this pill, what happens when a stateful streaming workload is forced onto a serverless, stateless architecture?",
    options: [
      "It runs a little slower but produces correct results",
      "It produces incorrect numbers quietly, one database query at a time, until someone downstream notices",
      "It fails loudly and immediately with a clear error",
      "It automatically converts itself into a Flink job"
    ],
    correct: 1,
    explanation: "The failure here is not a crash, it is a quiet accumulation of wrong numbers, which is exactly what makes forcing the wrong paradigm dangerous rather than just inefficient."
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Quiz: Three Questions Before You Design a Streaming System | Streaming Pill 9"
  description="Test whether you can run the closing checklist: is it stateful, what is the late-data tolerance, and can you trust the clocks."
>
  <section class="pill-quiz-page">
    <div class="container">
      <a href="/blog/streaming-pill-9-three-questions" class="back-link">← Back to Pill 9</a>

      <div class="pill-header">
        <div class="badge badge-series" style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">Pill 9 · Three Questions</div>
        <h1>Quiz: Run the Checklist</h1>
        <p class="pill-lead">
          These questions test whether you can apply the three-question framework to a new
          use case, not just recite the questions in order.
        </p>
      </div>

      <Quiz
        title="Pill 9: Three Questions Before You Design a Streaming System"
        questions={questions}
      />

      <div class="pill-footer glass-card">
        <h3>Series complete!</h3>
        <p>
          You have reached the end of the Streaming Pills series. If you worked through all
          10 pills and their quizzes, you now have a checklist you can run against any
          "should this be a stream?" question: whether it needs state, what its tolerance for
          late data is, and whether its clocks can be trusted.
        </p>
        <p>
          The goal was never to memorize Flink APIs. It was to build the reasoning that tells
          you, for a specific use case, which side of the batch and streaming boundary it falls
          on, and why.
        </p>
        <div class="pill-footer-links">
          <a href="/blog/streaming-pill-9-three-questions" class="btn btn-secondary btn-sm">Re-read Pill 9</a>
          <a href="/blog/streaming-pills" class="btn btn-primary btn-sm">Back to the full series</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .pill-quiz-page {
    padding: 3.5rem 0 5rem;
  }

  .back-link {
    display: inline-block;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    margin-bottom: 1.8rem;
    transition: color 0.2s ease;
  }
  .back-link:hover {
    color: var(--accent-blue);
  }

  .badge-series {
    background: var(--accent-blue);
    color: #ffffff;
  }

  .pill-header {
    max-width: 720px;
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
  }

  .pill-header h1 {
    font-size: clamp(1.6rem, 3.5vw, 2.2rem);
    margin-bottom: 0.8rem;
    line-height: 1.3;
  }

  .pill-lead {
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    line-height: 1.7;
    max-width: 640px;
  }

  .pill-footer {
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
    padding: 2rem;
    background: var(--bg-card);
  }

  .pill-footer h3 {
    font-size: 1.2rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
    color: var(--text-main);
  }

  .pill-footer p {
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
    margin-bottom: 1.1rem;
    max-width: 600px;
  }

  .pill-footer-links {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.65rem;
  }
</style>
